---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, profilePageSchema, organizationSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_FOUNDED } from '../../data/site-config';
import { P } from '../../lib/tables';
const lang = 'en';
const title = 'Who Publishes NZSalary.com, How It Is Funded and Checked';
const description = 'NZSalary.com is published by Radif Partners: why we built a PAYE, KiwiSaver and ACC calculator for New Zealand, how it is funded and how to report an error.';
const jsonLd = [profilePageSchema({ path: route('about', lang), lang }), organizationSchema(lang), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path: route('about', lang) }])];
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={jsonLd} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]} showAuthor={false}>
  <header class="mb-6"><h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">About this site</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none" data-author="Radif Partners">
    <p>NZSalary.com is published by <strong>Radif Partners</strong>, an independent publisher of free payroll and tax calculators for several countries. The site was started in {SITE_FOUNDED} to give New Zealand employees, contractors and employers a pay calculator that follows Inland Revenue’s rules for the 2026-27 tax year line by line, including the parts most calculators skip: the new 3.5 % KiwiSaver default, ESCT on employer contributions, the independent earner tax credit and its abatement, secondary tax codes and the ACC levy ceiling.</p>
    <h2>Why this calculator exists</h2>
    <p>Three things changed in New Zealand pay in less than two years: the tax thresholds moved on 31 July 2024, the government KiwiSaver contribution was halved in July 2025, and the default KiwiSaver rate rose to 3.5 % in April 2026. Many calculators still mix old and new rules. We rebuilt the calculation from Inland Revenue’s rates, checked each parameter against the official page, and show the source and date next to every figure.</p>
    <h2>From an Inland Revenue page to your take-home figure</h2>
    <p>Tax bands, secondary code rates, the ACC earners’ levy and its ceiling, KiwiSaver and ESCT thresholds, the student loan threshold: each value the engine uses sits in one file of 2026-27 figures, alongside the list of {Object.keys(P.sources).length} official pages it was taken from. Calculators, tables and guides all draw on that file through the same engine, so none of them carries a rate typed by hand. The file was last reconciled with the official pages on {P.retrieved_at}.</p>
    <p>For the formulas, the test cases and what is not modelled (Working for Families, tailored tax codes, the self-employed ACC work levy), see the <a href={route('method', lang)}>methodology page</a>. Sourcing and corrections are covered by the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>Funding and your data</h2>
    <p>Using NZSalary.com costs nothing and needs no login. Because PAYE, KiwiSaver and student loan deductions are worked out on your own device, we never see what you enter, which leaves no data to sell. The site carries no ads today; any added later would go beneath the calculators, not within them. KiwiSaver scheme providers, banks, employers and government agencies neither fund the site nor review what it says.</p>
    <h2>What we are not</h2>
    <p>We are not tax agents and not affiliated with Inland Revenue, ACC or MBIE. The results are estimates to help you plan; your payslip and your end-of-year income tax assessment are the documents that count.</p>
    <h2>Report an error</h2>
    <p>Think a PAYE, ACC or KiwiSaver figure is off? Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with the page address, the pay, tax code and options you chose, and the amount you expected. We rework the case from the Inland Revenue rule behind it and, where we got it wrong, correct the site and date the fix.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
